<section class="vx-section" aria-labelledby="guard-title">
  <div class="vx-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Experimental Guard</div>
    <h2 id="guard-title">Explicit, npm-only, and deliberately narrow</h2>
    <pre class="vx-pre"><code><span class="code-prompt">$</span> <span class="code-cmd">vexes guard --</span> <span class="code-arg">npm install package-name</span></code></pre>
    <p>Guard resolves a proposed public-registry npm change in a disposable project copy with lifecycle scripts disabled, assesses the changed lockfile occurrences, and applies an approved graph with <code>--ignore-scripts</code>. It refuses pnpm, Yarn, local/git/tarball specs, workspaces, arbitrary npm flags, and shell-wrapper setup. It binds approval to lockfile metadata; it does not verify bytes later written to <code>node_modules</code>, and a failed install can leave that directory partially changed.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="vx-section" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
  <div class="vx-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Trust Boundary</div>
    <h2 id="limits-title">Reasons not to over-trust it</h2>
    <ul class="vx-security-list" role="list">
      <li><span class="vx-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span><span class="vx-security-text"><strong>Heuristics miss and false-positive.</strong> A quiet result is not evidence that a package is benign.</span></li>
      <li><span class="vx-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span><span class="vx-security-text"><strong>Analysis coverage varies.</strong> OSV covers the documented ecosystems; bounded source and registry heuristics are currently npm/PyPI only.</span></li>
      <li><span class="vx-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span><span class="vx-security-text"><strong>Optional sandbox telemetry is not a malware lab.</strong> It is Linux <code>bwrap</code>-only, opt-in, partial, and refused when the accepted boundary is unavailable.</span></li>
      <li><span class="vx-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span><span class="vx-security-text"><strong>No external audit or live efficacy evidence.</strong> The benchmark is reproducible evidence of its bounded checks, not a claim that it would have prevented a named incident.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
